<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Clazomenae</h1><p class="st-meta">1000 BCE – 133 BCE (approx.) · capital Clazomenae</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Ionian city on the Gulf of Smyrna, birthplace of the philosopher Anaxagoras, which moved onto an offshore islet to escape Persian attack.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Clazomenae was founded by Ionian settlers, traditionally from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colophon">Colophon</a>, on the southern shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-izmir">Gulf of Smyrna</a> c. 1000 BCE. It joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ionian-league">Ionian League</a> and in the 7th century BCE helped found <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/abdera">Abdera</a>, a first attempt later renewed by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/teos">Teos</a>, and traded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naucratis">Naucratis</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Clazomenae was known for painted terracotta sarcophagi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-revolt">Ionian Revolt</a> its people moved to an islet just offshore for safety. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anaxagoras">Anaxagoras</a>, who taught that mind orders the cosmos, was born there c. 500 BCE. It belonged to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delian-league">Delian League</a> and was fought over during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-antalcidas">King's Peace</a> of 387 BCE named Clazomenae as Persian territory. Alexander linked the island to the mainland by a causeway. Under Hellenistic kings and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, from 133 BCE in the province of Asia, it slowly declined.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/clazomenae.jpg" alt="Clazomenae" width="320" height="157"><figcaption>Image: Megistias · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ionia499bc.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1000 BCE – 133 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Clazomenae</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ionic Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Turkey</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Klazomenai</dd></dl></aside></div>

